[{:db/id -1, :adr/id "2607050900-kotoba-lang-naming-audit.edn", :adr/normalization-status :raw-preserved, :adr/normalization-error "Doesn't support namespace:  — the decisive test is genericity/parameterization, not\n  whether a library's *subject matter* (witness selection, quorum,\n  attestation) sounds agent-flavored.\n- `witness-quorum`'s own README already states this directly: ", :adr/raw-edn "{:frontmatter\n {:id \"adr-2607050900-kotoba-lang-naming-audit\"\n  :title \"ADR-2607050900: kotoba-lang org naming audit -- IPLD/Datomic/IPNS/IPFS/Clojure-stdlib clusters\"\n  :status \"accepted\"\n  :date \"2026-07-05\"\n  :deciders [\"Jun Kawasaki\"]\n  :doc_type \"adr\"\n  :topic \"kotoba-lang-naming-audit\"\n  :authoritative true\n  :authoritative_for\n  [\"Scoped naming/duplication audit of kotoba-lang org repos centered on IPLD/Datomic/IPNS/IPFS/Clojure-stdlib conventions, as of 2026-07-05\"\n   \"Confirms no functional/dependency-level duplication found in any investigated cluster -- every confusable-name pair turned out to be genuinely distinct in scope once its own README was read\"\n   \"Confirms the kotodama/kototama romanization split is real, undocumented on either side, and is the clearest naming-drift finding\"\n   \"Confirms kotoba-client/kotobase-client/kotobase are three genuinely distinct, correctly-scoped repos with a naming-surface collision (no repo cross-references the other two)\"\n   \"No renames or documentation edits executed by this document -- audit only, follow-up items listed but not authorized\"]\n  :supersedes []\n  :superseded_by []\n  :related [\"adr-2607050700\" \"adr-2607050800\" \"adr-2607050500\" \"adr-2606302300\"]\n  :last_verified \"2026-07-05\"\n  :artifacts {}}\n\n :clusters\n {:healthy\n  [{:repos [\"ipld\" \"dag-cbor\" \"multiformats\" \"prolly-tree\" \"arrangement\" \"datom\" \"kotobase-peer\" \"chain\"]\n    :why \"Content-addressing + Datomic-parallel core; already covered by ADR-2607050700/2607050800. Consistent vocabulary, no RDF-flavored residue, no overlap.\"}\n   {:repos [\"ipns\" \"did\"]\n    :why \"ipns's README explicitly states it's \\\"the naming half of the same discipline kotoba-lang/did covers for did:key -- siblings derived from the same raw pubkey, kept separate because they are different specs,\\\" and notes it replaced duplicated derivation code previously in kekkai/cacao.clj and kagi/identity.clj. Exemplary cross-documentation.\"}\n   {:repos [\"lint\" \"lint-kotoba\"]\n    :why \"lint-kotoba's README states directly: lint formats/parses, lint-kotoba is the source-level static analysis between lint and wit's over-grant checking, and depends on lint. Zero ambiguity despite near-identical names.\"}\n   {:repos [\"mst\" \"atproto\"]\n    :why \"mst explicitly scopes to pure MST primitives only, stating the byte/CID/CAR layer belongs in atproto.\"}\n   {:repos [\"ipfs\" \"atproto-client\"]\n    :why \"atproto-client's README explicitly contrasts its own IHttp port design against ipfs's (single -request vs -get/-post/-post-file; string vs byte bodies), reasoned from XRPC vs Kubo needs -- a stated design comparison, not silent duplication.\"}\n   {:repos [\"witness-quorum\"]\n    :why \"Positive precedent, not a problem: README states it was deliberately named witness-quorum instead of echoing its original directory name kotoba-datomic, \\\"to describe what it actually does and avoid echoing the name of the now-deleted Rust crate\\\" it was mistakenly assumed to reimplement. Same naming discipline as ADR-2607050700/2607050800, evidence the practice already exists elsewhere.\"}]\n\n  :naming-drift\n  [{:repos [\"kotodama\" \"kotodama-cells\" \"kotodama-holochain\" \"kotodama-host\" \"kotodama-mcp\" \"kotodama-py\" \"kototama\" \"kototama-cljc-contract\"]\n    :issue \"Undocumented romanization split: kotodama and kototama are two different romanizations of 言霊 (\\\"word-spirit\\\"), thematically on-brand for \\\"kotoba\\\" (言葉, \\\"word/language\\\") -- which makes confusion MORE likely, not less.\"\n    :evidence \"kotodama's README mentions \\\"kototama\\\" exactly once, as etzhayyim/kototama (a DIFFERENT GitHub org's repo), cited as historical origin only. kotoba-lang/kototama's own README never mentions kotodama at all. Neither side explains why both spellings persist as separate, non-cross-referenced families.\"\n    :severity :high\n    :recommendation \"Add a short cross-reference note to both READMEs (mirror ipns's own pattern) explaining the spelling split and each repo's actual scope relative to the other.\"}\n   {:repos [\"kotoba-client\" \"kotobase-client\" \"kotobase\"]\n    :issue \"Three similarly named, genuinely different repos with no explicit disambiguation between them.\"\n    :evidence \"kotobase = server-side IStore port seam. kotobase-client = CACAO-authed client for the kotobase.net tenant Datom plane (de-forks code formerly hand-copied into app-aozora/app-aozora-boundary/kami-genko). kotoba-client = generic, non-CACAO CID-verified block ingest/hydrate client over kotoba's content graph, consumed by p2p. None of the three READMEs names or contrasts itself against the other two.\"\n    :severity :high\n    :recommendation \"Add a one-line disambiguation section to all three READMEs cross-referencing each other, same pattern atproto-client already uses against ipfs.\"}]\n\n  :stale-or-low-value\n  [{:repos [\"kotoba-v2025\"]\n    :issue \"Self-described \\\"Legacy Reference\\\" -- historical Rust workspace (JSON-LD + OWL inference via a \\\"fukurow\\\" reasoner, Kernel/Actor/Mediator \\\"semanticos\\\" pattern), an abandoned design distinct from the active CLJC substrate. Own README correctly redirects to kotoba-lang/kotoba and kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang.\"\n    :evidence \"Appears TWICE in manifest/repos.edn's :extra-projects list (line ~79 and line ~563) -- harmless today (render() de-duplicates via distinct) but a repos.edn hygiene bug.\"\n    :severity :low\n    :recommendation \"Dedupe the repeated line next time repos.edn is touched for an unrelated reason; no urgency.\"}\n   {:repos [\"kotoba-adapter-contracts\" \"kototama-cljc-contract\" \"kotoba-lang-cli-contract\"]\n    :issue \"Three scaffold repos share the EXACT SAME one-line placeholder README: \\\"KAMI clj-wgsl migration Phase 4 home (ADR-2607010930) -- scaffold, Wave-2 port lands here.\\\"\"\n    :evidence \"May be intentional (three genuinely distinct landing zones for the same migration wave, not yet populated) or may indicate scaffolds created ahead of need that never got differentiated -- can't tell from the outside which, if any, is still an active target.\"\n    :severity :low\n    :recommendation \"Confirm whether all three are still live migration targets or can be retired/merged.\"\n    :investigated-2026-07-08\n    {:finding \"All three are size:0 GitHub repos (nothing beyond the placeholder README ever pushed). None of the three names, nor the term \\\"Wave-2,\\\" appears anywhere in ADR-2607010930's .md/.edn -- that migration has since progressed through Phase 7 (113/116 kami-engine crates restored) by a different route, never populating these three. kotoba-adapter-contracts's naming pattern IS a real, live convention elsewhere (kotoba-core-contracts, kotoba-selfhost-contracts are both real populated repos) -- reads as a legitimate scaffold that never got filled in, not a naming mistake.\"\n     :bug-found-and-fixed \"kototama-cljc-contract's and kotoba-lang-cli-contract's local west checkouts had git origin misconfigured to kotoba-lang/kototama.git and kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang.git respectively (unrelated active repos) -- a future push from either directory would have silently landed scaffold content in the wrong repo. Checked-out content itself was verified correct (identical to each repo's real origin/main); only the remote URL was wrong. Corrected both.\"\n     :recommendation \"Retire all three -- not executed here (repo archival/deletion is more consequential and less reversible than this pass's fixes; left as an explicit owner-confirmable follow-up). Re-scaffold fresh against the current migration plan if the scopes are still wanted.\"}}]\n\n  :placement-questions\n  [{:repos [\"witness-quorum\"]\n    :issue \"Own README self-identifies as @etzhayyim/witness-quorum, relocated from etzhayyim/root. Per repos.edn's org taxonomy (ADR-2606302300): kotoba-lang=language-substrate/platform, etzhayyim=agent-centric/public-interest. Deterministic witness selection + quorum + Ed25519 attestation reads as agent-centric/public-interest infra, not core language substrate.\"\n    :severity :medium\n    :recommendation \"Revisit org placement (kotoba-lang vs etzhayyim) against ADR-2606302300's taxonomy -- independent of the repo's (good) name, which is not in question.\"\n    :resolved-2026-07-08\n    {:conclusion \"No move needed -- kotoba-lang placement is correct.\"\n     :reasoning \"ADR-2606302300's actual test is genericity/parameterization (\\\"language substrate, consumed by all orgs\\\"), not domain-name intuition. witness-quorum's own README already states \\\"Zero etzhayyim-specific coupling beyond an NSID string constant and a 'council' escalation label -- every fleet topology, membrane rule, and signer is a caller-supplied parameter,\\\" and its real target consumer is kotoba-lang/murakumo (Murakumo babashka cell-runners), not an etzhayyim actor -- matching the reasoning that justified its original 2026-07-01 relocation FROM etzhayyim TO kotoba-lang. Surface-level domain vibes don't override the taxonomy's actual criterion.\"}}]\n\n  :noted-not-a-problem\n  [{:repos [\"kotoba-lang\"]\n    :note \"Repo shares its name with the org itself. README explains the split clearly (\\\"split from kotoba-lang/kotoba so the language surface can be reviewed independently from the current compiler/runtime/server/mesh implementation\\\") -- documented, not accidental. Flagged only as a standing minor discoverability friction (searching \\\"kotoba-lang\\\" can land on the wrong result), not a naming-quality defect.\"}\n   {:repos [\"kotoba-topology\"]\n    :note \"An arXiv-style LaTeX paper (\\\"Languages as Graph Topologies,\\\" cross-linguistic syntax analysis) sharing kotoba/topology-adjacent naming with the software org. No stated relationship to any other repo -- reads as an unrelated academic artifact, not a naming conflict with any specific software component.\"}\n   {:repos [\"sql\"]\n    :note \"\\\"Kotoba DSL package for kotoba.sql... a compatibility facade\\\" over sql.core. Exists alongside rdf/sparql/shacl as another query-vocabulary member; not mis-named given SQL is its own real, distinct vocabulary (not competing with the Datomic-over-RDF priority), but worth remembering next time query-layer naming is revisited.\"}]}\n\n :body \"# ADR-2607050900: kotoba-lang org naming audit — IPLD/Datomic/IPNS/IPFS/Clojure-stdlib clusters\n\n**Status**: accepted (audit; no renames executed by this document)\n**Date**: 2026-07-05 (session-numbered; see repos.edn ADR numbering convention)\n**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki\n\n## Context\n\nFollowing ADR-2607050700/2607050800's rename work (RDF-flavored/generic\nnames → Datomic vocabulary), the owner asked for a wider sweep: across\nthe whole `kotoba-lang` GitHub org (415 registered repos as of this\naudit), are there other repos whose naming has drifted from the\nIPLD/Datomic/IPNS/IPFS/Clojure conventions this substrate has been\nconverging on, or that duplicate/overlap each other?\n\nMethod: every repo's own README was fetched and read directly (not\ninferred from the name alone) for the candidate clusters below — the\ncontent-addressing core, the Datomic-parallel database layer, the\nidentity/naming layer, and the foundational Clojure stdlib. The ~350\nremaining repos (3D/robotics `kami-*` engine family, ISIC industry-\nvertical libs, format/codec libs unrelated to any of the five themes)\nwere **not** individually audited — this is a scoped sweep, not an\norg-wide census.\n\n## Findings\n\nSee the `:clusters` map above this body for the structured data. In\nprose:\n\n### Healthy — no action needed\n\nContent-addressing core (`ipld`/`dag-cbor`/`multiformats`/`prolly-tree`/\n`arrangement`/`datom`/`kotobase-peer`/`chain`), `ipns`↔`did`,\n`lint`↔`lint-kotoba`, `mst`↔`atproto`, `ipfs`↔`atproto-client`, and\n`witness-quorum` (a positive precedent for deliberate naming discipline)\nall show either clean vocabulary alignment or exemplary cross-\ndocumentation between neighbors.\n\n### Naming drift / confusability (real, not yet acted on)\n\n`kotodama*` vs `kototama*` — an undocumented romanization split of 言霊\nacross 7 repos, unexplained on either side. `kotoba-client` /\n`kotobase-client` / `kotobase` — three genuinely distinct, correctly-\nscoped repos with a naming-surface collision and zero cross-referencing.\n\n### Likely stale / low-value duplication\n\n`kotoba-v2025` (correctly self-labeled legacy, but duplicated as a\nmanifest list entry). Three scaffold repos sharing identical placeholder\nREADME text.\n\n### Placement question\n\n`witness-quorum`'s residence in `kotoba-lang` vs its self-identified\n`@etzhayyim/` scope, against the org's own role/scope taxonomy\n(ADR-2606302300).\n\n### Noted but not a problem\n\n`kotoba-lang` (repo) sharing a name with the org itself (documented,\njust an ergonomic friction). `kotoba-topology` (an unrelated academic\npaper repo). `sql` (a legitimate, distinct query vocabulary alongside\n`rdf`/`sparql`/`shacl`).\n\n## Consequences\n\n- (+) Two exemplary cross-documentation patterns already exist in the\n  org (`ipns`↔`did`, `lint`↔`lint-kotoba`) — the fix for the\n  `kotodama`/`kototama` and `kotoba-client`/`kotobase-client`/`kotobase`\n  findings isn't a new practice, it's applying an existing one.\n- (+) No functional/dependency-level duplication was found — every\n  cluster investigated turned out to implement genuinely different\n  scope once its README was actually read, including the ones with\n  confusable names. This audit found a *documentation* gap, not a\n  *duplicated-effort* gap.\n- (−) `kotodama`/`kototama` is a real, unresolved point of confusion for\n  anyone new to the org — this audit does not resolve it (no rename\n  executed), it only confirms the drift and that neither side explains\n  it.\n- (±) The `kotoba-v2025` duplicate list entry and the three identical-\n  boilerplate scaffold repos are low-severity hygiene items, not naming\n  decisions — left as noted findings rather than acted on, since fixing\n  them wasn't the ask.\n\n## Follow-up (recommended, not yet authorized)\n\n- Add a short cross-reference note to `kotodama`'s and `kototama`'s\n  READMEs (mirroring `ipns`'s own pattern) explaining the spelling\n  split and each repo's actual scope relative to the other.\n- Add a one-line disambiguation section to `kotobase`, `kotobase-client`,\n  and `kotoba-client`'s READMEs cross-referencing the other two (same\n  pattern `atproto-client` already uses against `ipfs`).\n- Dedupe the `kotoba-v2025` line in `manifest/repos.edn`'s\n  `:extra-projects` (minimal diff, `--entry` not needed since it's a\n  pure list-hygiene fix, not a pin change).\n- Confirm whether the three identical-boilerplate scaffold repos\n  (`kotoba-adapter-contracts`, `kototama-cljc-contract`,\n  `kotoba-lang-cli-contract`) are still live migration targets or can be\n  retired/merged.\n- Revisit `witness-quorum`'s org placement (`kotoba-lang` vs\n  `etzhayyim`) against ADR-2606302300's taxonomy.\n\n## Follow-up (2026-07-08) — scaffold-repo status investigated\n\nInvestigated whether `kotoba-adapter-contracts`, `kototama-cljc-contract`,\nand `kotoba-lang-cli-contract` are still live migration targets:\n\n- All three exist as their own correctly-named GitHub repos, but each is\n  `\\\"size\\\":0` — nothing beyond the identical placeholder README was ever\n  pushed. No `deps.edn`, no `src`.\n- The placeholder text cites \\\"KAMI clj-wgsl migration Phase 4... Wave-2\n  port lands here\\\" (ADR-2607010930), but none of the three repo names\n  appears anywhere in that ADR's `.md` or `.edn` (grepped both), and\n  \\\"Wave-2\\\" as a term doesn't appear in it either — the only \\\"wave\\\"\n  language there is Phase 7's unrelated \\\"ambitious-scope wave closed:\n  113/116 kami-engine crates restored.\\\" The migration that ADR tracks has\n  since progressed through Phase 7 by a different route, without ever\n  populating these three.\n- `kotoba-adapter-contracts`'s naming pattern (`kotoba-*-contracts`) is a\n  real, live convention elsewhere in the org — `kotoba-core-contracts` and\n  `kotoba-selfhost-contracts` are both real, populated CLJC/EDN-authority\n  repos — so this one specifically reads as a legitimate scaffold that\n  simply never got filled in, not a naming mistake.\n- **Found and fixed a real, separate bug while investigating**: the local\n  west checkouts for `kototama-cljc-contract` and `kotoba-lang-cli-contract`\n  had their git `origin` remotes misconfigured to point at `kotoba-lang/\n  kototama.git` and `kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang.git` respectively (unrelated,\n  actively-developed repos) instead of their own repos. The checked-out\n  content itself was correct (verified identical to each repo's real\n  `origin/main`), so no data was at risk from this session's use, but a\n  future `git push` from either directory would have silently pushed\n  scaffold content into the wrong (real, active) repo. Corrected both\n  remote URLs; re-verified fetch and content match.\n\n**Recommendation (not executed here): retire all three** — archiving or\ndeleting a GitHub repo is a more consequential, less reversible action\nthan this pass's fixes, so it's left as an explicit, owner-confirmable\nfollow-up rather than done unilaterally. If the `adapter-contracts` /\n`cli-contract` scopes are still wanted, they should be re-scaffolded\nfresh against the *current* migration plan rather than reusing these\nempty, stale-citation placeholders.\n\n## Follow-up (2026-07-08) — witness-quorum org-placement question resolved\n\nRe-examined `witness-quorum`'s residence in `kotoba-lang` against\nADR-2606302300's actual placement criteria, rather than domain-name\nintuition:\n\n- ADR-2606302300 defines `kotoba-lang` as \"language substrate, consumed\n  by all orgs\" — the decisive test is genericity/parameterization, not\n  whether a library's *subject matter* (witness selection, quorum,\n  attestation) sounds agent-flavored.\n- `witness-quorum`'s own README already states this directly: \"Zero\n  etzhayyim-specific coupling beyond an NSID string constant and a\n  \"council\" escalation label — every fleet topology, membrane rule,\n  and signer is a caller-supplied parameter.\" `WitnessTransport` is\n  \"a documented plain map of functions,\" and the production HTTP/PDS\n  transport was *deliberately not ported* so the package doesn't\n  hard-code any one deployment's shape.\n- Its stated real target consumer is \"Murakumo babashka cell-runners\" —\n  `kotoba-lang/murakumo`, itself a `kotoba-lang` cross-cutting\n  infra repo, not an `etzhayyim` actor.\n- This matches — and was the actual justification for — its 2026-07-01\n  relocation *from* `etzhayyim/root` *to* `kotoba-lang` in the first\n  place (per the README's own \"Provenance\" section), applying\n  ADR-2606302300's rule as designed.\n\n**Conclusion: no move needed.** The naming-audit ADR's placement\nquestion is resolved as \"already correctly placed\" — surface-level\ndomain vibes (witness/quorum/attestation \"sounds\" agent-centric) don't\noverride the taxonomy's actual test (generic + parameterized + already\nconsumed by kotoba-lang's own infra). This closes the last open\nfollow-up from this ADR.\n\n## One-line summary\n\n**A scoped naming sweep of `kotoba-lang`'s IPLD/Datomic/IPNS/IPFS/\nClojure-stdlib clusters found no functional duplication — every\nconfusable-looking pair, once its README was actually read, turned out\nto be genuinely distinct — but two real documentation gaps: the\nundocumented `kotodama`/`kototama` romanization split, and the\n`kotoba-client`/`kotobase-client`/`kotobase` naming-surface collision,\nneither of which cross-references its neighbors the way `ipns`↔`did` and\n`lint`↔`lint-kotoba` already exemplarily do. A `kotoba-v2025` duplicate\nmanifest entry, three identically-boilerplated scaffold repos, and\n`witness-quorum`'s org placement are noted as minor, lower-priority\nhygiene items.**\n\"}\n"}]
